3674922,"stop":"eos","text":"Let's denote the whole number as \\( x \\). The next four consecutive whole numbers will be \\( x+1, x+2, x+3, \\) and \\( x+4 \\). The sum of these five numbers is given as 105. We can write the equation for the sum as follows:\n\n\\[\nx + (x+1) + (x+2) + (x+3) + (x+4) = 105\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n5x + 10 = 105\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( x \\) by isolating it on one side of the equation. Subtract 10 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n5x = 95\n\\]\n\nThen, divide both sides by 5:\n\n\\[\nx = 19\n\\]\n\nSo, the five consecutive whole numbers are \\( 19, 20, 21, 22, \\) and \\( 23 \\).\n\nThe median of these five numbers is the middle number, which is 21. The mean of these five numbers is the sum of the numbers divided by 5:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{19 + 20 + 21 + 22 + 23}{5} = \\frac{105}{5} = 21\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the result when the mean is subtracted from the median. Since the mean and the median are both 21, the result is:\n\n\\[\n21 - 21 = 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:503"}
